WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.950 --> 00:00:05.419
Welcome to Nightjar, your own private studio for writing your novel.

2
00:00:05.469 --> 00:00:11.068
Everything you write stays safely on your computer, and it saves by itself as you go.

3
00:00:11.118 --> 00:00:12.596
Let me show you around.

4
00:00:12.646 --> 00:00:16.783
We'll use a finished example book called The Salt House, so you

5
00:00:16.833 --> 00:00:18.710
can see everything in action.

6
00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:21.563
This is your Home page.

7
00:00:21.613 --> 00:00:25.415
You can see your title, how many words you've written, and today's

8
00:00:25.465 --> 00:00:27.283
progress toward your daily goal.

9
00:00:27.333 --> 00:00:31.603
The big button takes you straight back to the chapter you were working on.

10
00:00:31.653 --> 00:00:36.390
And whenever you're unsure what to do next, the Guide button shows your next step.

11
00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:39.854
The Guide is your map.

12
00:00:39.904 --> 00:00:43.844
It shows the whole journey of writing a book, in the order

13
00:00:43.894 --> 00:00:48.798
that works best: the heart of your story, your characters, your mystery,

14
00:00:48.848 --> 00:00:51.963
planning, writing, polishing, and your ending.

15
00:00:52.013 --> 00:00:54.439
Steps tick themselves off as you go.

16
00:00:54.489 --> 00:00:57.742
Click Take me there, and a bar at the top of the

17
00:00:57.792 --> 00:01:01.870
page always lets you come back, or move on to the next step.

18
00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:06.353
Below the steps is the Craft corner.

19
00:01:06.403 --> 00:01:10.938
These are short, friendly lessons on things like point of view, planting

20
00:01:10.988 --> 00:01:13.167
clues fairly, and writing dialogue.

21
00:01:13.217 --> 00:01:16.670
Each one has a Try it button that opens the right tool.

22
00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:23.869
The Story Bible holds the big picture: your premise, your setting, and

23
00:01:23.919 --> 00:01:25.324
the feel of the book.

24
00:01:25.374 --> 00:01:29.619
On the Tone tab, simple sliders tell your editor how dark, how

25
00:01:29.669 --> 00:01:31.974
romantic, or how fast you want it.

26
00:01:32.024 --> 00:01:36.685
On the Decisions tab, every idea is marked either Decided, or Maybe.

27
00:01:36.735 --> 00:01:41.950
Your editor treats decided things as fact, and never treats a maybe as fact.

28
00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:47.269
Also on the Tone tab: the books and authors you love.

29
00:01:47.319 --> 00:01:50.211
Name them, and say what you love about each one.

30
00:01:50.261 --> 00:01:53.828
Then one click turns them into a style guide for your book.

31
00:01:53.878 --> 00:01:58.180
Your editor learns the craft behind them, never their words, and blends

32
00:01:58.230 --> 00:01:59.590
it with your own voice.

33
00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:06.793
In Characters, each person has a profile: what they want, what they

34
00:02:06.843 --> 00:02:09.690
fear, what they hide, and how they talk.

35
00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:12.369
You don't need to fill everything in.

36
00:02:12.419 --> 00:02:16.425
And here's a lovely tool: you can interview a character.

37
00:02:16.475 --> 00:02:20.554
Ask Owen where he was the night Tess died, and he answers

38
00:02:20.604 --> 00:02:24.030
in his own voice, and he lies, just as he would.

39
00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:28.985
The Mystery board keeps your puzzle straight.

40
00:02:29.035 --> 00:02:33.663
Clues are colour coded: green for genuine clues, and red for red herrings.

41
00:02:33.713 --> 00:02:38.911
The Suspects tab tracks motive, means and opportunity for everyone under suspicion.

42
00:02:38.961 --> 00:02:42.704
And Connect the dots lets you pick any pieces of your story,

43
00:02:42.754 --> 00:02:44.790
and ask how they could be linked.

44
00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:52.732
The Timeline shows when things really happened, including events before the book begins.

45
00:02:52.782 --> 00:02:55.910
If someone is in two places at once, it warns you.

46
00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:03.936
In Scenes and Outline, you plan each chapter by answering simple questions.

47
00:03:03.986 --> 00:03:04.733
What happens?

48
00:03:04.783 --> 00:03:05.897
What does she want?

49
00:03:05.947 --> 00:03:07.184
What gets in the way?

50
00:03:07.234 --> 00:03:09.696
What should the reader wonder afterwards?

51
00:03:09.746 --> 00:03:14.170
Then one click turns your answers into a chapter plan you can write from.

52
00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:18.084
Facing a blank chapter?

53
00:03:18.134 --> 00:03:20.288
Click Draft this chapter for me.

54
00:03:20.338 --> 00:03:25.041
Your editor writes a full first draft from your plan, your characters

55
00:03:25.091 --> 00:03:27.107
and your clues, in your style.

56
00:03:27.157 --> 00:03:31.102
Then it edits its own draft, the way a professional would.

57
00:03:31.152 --> 00:03:34.408
You read it first, and if you like it, put it in

58
00:03:34.458 --> 00:03:36.750
your chapter and make it your own.

59
00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:41.861
Now, the writing room.

60
00:03:41.911 --> 00:03:44.980
It's a quiet page with your chapters on the left.

61
00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:45.681
Just write.

62
00:03:45.731 --> 00:03:49.628
It saves automatically, and your word count updates as you go.

63
00:03:49.678 --> 00:03:52.620
The Talk button lets you speak instead of type.

64
00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:58.662
When you want to polish a passage, select it with your mouse,

65
00:03:58.712 --> 00:04:00.170
and a small menu appears.

66
00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:02.826
Choose Improve, and pick how much to change.

67
00:04:02.876 --> 00:04:06.930
Your editor suggests a revision, and shows you exactly what changed.

68
00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:12.060
Nothing changes unless you click Accept, and your original is always kept in History.

69
00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:17.399
Under the More menu, there are a few more helpers.

70
00:04:17.449 --> 00:04:20.973
Check my prose is free and instant, and flags tired phrases.

71
00:04:21.023 --> 00:04:24.846
Read it like a reader gives you an honest first reader's reaction

72
00:04:24.896 --> 00:04:28.241
to your chapter: what gripped them, and who they suspect.

73
00:04:28.291 --> 00:04:32.650
And Update my story bible adds new facts from your chapter with one click.

74
00:04:34.390 --> 00:04:36.048
On the right is your editor.

75
00:04:36.098 --> 00:04:39.402
Choose Think, and describe a problem in your own words.

76
00:04:39.452 --> 00:04:43.488
You'll get a few ideas, each with why it works, and its weaknesses.

77
00:04:43.538 --> 00:04:46.720
Click Use this to make it part of your story, Save as

78
00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:51.660
possibility to keep it for later, or Not for me, and it won't be suggested again.

79
00:04:53.390 --> 00:04:56.813
On the Ending page, describe how your story resolves.

80
00:04:56.863 --> 00:04:59.500
Then click Plan backwards from my ending.

81
00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:03.431
Your editor works out what the reader needs to see along the

82
00:05:03.481 --> 00:05:06.970
way, and suggests which chapter each piece belongs in.

83
00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:12.071
Once you have a few chapters, visit the Story Lab.

84
00:05:12.121 --> 00:05:15.839
The page-turner analysis reads your book the way a reader would, and

85
00:05:15.889 --> 00:05:19.995
charts where they'd happily keep reading, and where they might put it down.

86
00:05:20.045 --> 00:05:24.040
It also shows the questions keeping readers hooked, and the emotional ups

87
00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:25.370
and downs of your story.

88
00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:32.831
The solvability test has three imaginary readers guess who did it after

89
00:05:32.881 --> 00:05:36.208
every chapter, so you can see when your mystery gets cracked.

90
00:05:36.258 --> 00:05:39.917
And the stress tests ask the tough questions, like what your killer

91
00:05:39.967 --> 00:05:44.180
would do to get away with it, and whether readers will root for your heroine.

92
00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:50.521
On the Polish page, the health report checks your book's length, your

93
00:05:50.571 --> 00:05:53.899
chapter lengths, and words you might be leaning on too often.

94
00:05:53.949 --> 00:05:55.117
It's free and instant.

95
00:05:55.167 --> 00:05:59.160
You can also keep a style sheet, check names, and proofread each chapter.

96
00:06:00.950 --> 00:06:04.481
When the book is ready, the Publish page helps with the business

97
00:06:04.531 --> 00:06:08.509
side: your blurb, synopsis and query letter, a panel of imaginary agents

98
00:06:08.559 --> 00:06:11.531
to test it on, a list to track the agents you contact,

99
00:06:11.581 --> 00:06:14.720
and a submission package formatted the way agents expect.

100
00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:21.800
And the Series page helps your book stand on its own while leaving room for more.

101
00:06:21.850 --> 00:06:27.640
When you're ready, one click starts book two, with all your characters and places carried over.

102
00:06:28.990 --> 00:06:33.084
Back on Home, Today's scene suggests one small thing to write each

103
00:06:33.134 --> 00:06:37.103
day, and your pace shows when your first draft will be finished.

104
00:06:37.153 --> 00:06:39.740
Little and often is how books get written.

105
00:06:41.510 --> 00:06:42.543
Finally, Settings.

106
00:06:42.593 --> 00:06:46.994
Choose a backup folder once, and your novel is copied there automatically.

107
00:06:47.044 --> 00:06:49.941
And when your book is ready, you can save it as a

108
00:06:49.991 --> 00:06:53.610
Word document in proper manuscript format, ready to send out.

109
00:06:55.230 --> 00:06:56.192
That's the tour.

110
00:06:56.242 --> 00:06:57.964
Remember: you're the author.

111
00:06:58.014 --> 00:07:02.584
Your editor is here to help you think and polish, but the story is yours.

112
00:07:02.634 --> 00:07:06.064
Whenever you're unsure what to do next, open the Guide.

113
00:07:06.114 --> 00:07:06.950
Happy writing.

